{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018RMrpcxaw9jNrrEQvcK8B9","type":"tool_result","content":"# ard — automated repository development\n\nard makes a GitHub repository maintain itself. Scanners file deduplicated issues, triage agents turn each issue into a plan, a worker agent implements it on a branch, reviewer agents critique the PR, and a merger lands it once every review passes.\n\n**Documentation:** <https://albertcbrown.github.io/ard/> (sources in [docs/site](docs/site)).\n\n> **Status:** under active development. The design lives in [docs/proposal.md](docs/proposal.md), and the work is tracked as [GitHub issues](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues) grouped into phase milestones.\n\n## How it works\n\nOne rule holds the system together: **a deterministic controller owns every GitHub and git side effect; agents only read and write files in a worktree.**\n\nOne `ard` process per repository runs six loops that share a small pool of job slots (default 3):\n\n| Loop | Every | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Scanners | 4 h | Find problems (CVEs, outdated components, lint, docs, missing tests…), file or update issues, close what is fixed |\n| Triage | 15 min | Role agents (lead, PM, architect, designer, doc planner, engineer) turn an issue into `plan.md` |\n| Workers | 5 min | Implement the plan on `worker/<role>-<issue#>-<slug>`, run tests, open a draft PR |\n| Reviewers | 5 min | Role agents (security, license, eng, PM) review the PR and request fixes |\n| Merger | 5 min | Squash-merge PRs whose reviews all passed and whose checks are green |\n| Reaper | 1 min | Mark dead or overdue jobs `ard:executor:failed`, keep their evidence 24 h |\n\nGitHub labels prefixed `ard:` are the queue and the state machine. `.ard/<issue#>/` in the target repository holds each issue's plan, per-role summaries and full agent logs.\n\n## Configuration by convention\n\nA target repository opts in with an `.ard/` directory. A role exists because its directory exists:\n\n```\n.ard/\n├── config.yaml          # global settings and overrides only\n├── scanners/<name>/     # scan (executable) or prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── triage/<name>/       # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── workers/<name>/      # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n├── reviewers/<name>/    # prompt.txt; optional Dockerfile, scripts\n└── <issue#>/            # per-issue output and logs, written only by ard\n```\n\nSee [Configuration](docs/proposal.md#configuration) in the proposal for the full schema and defaults.\n\n## Development\n\nRequires Go (see `go.mod`).\n\n```bash\nmake build   # builds bin/ard\nmake test    # go test ./...\nmake lint    # go vet and a gofmt check\n```\n\nContributor and agent guidance is in [CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md).\n\n## License\n\n[GPL-3.0](LICENSE)\n# Getting started\n\nThis takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` commands from a clone of the target repository, or pass `--repo owner/name`.\n\n## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n\nard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. Create an App you own and give it exactly these repository permissions:\n\n| Permission | Access |\n| --- | --- |\n| Contents | Read and write |\n| Issues | Read and write |\n| Pull requests | Read and write |\n| Checks | Read and write |\n| Commit statuses | Read |\n| Metadata | Read |\n\nDo **not** grant `workflows`. Without it GitHub itself rejects any push that edits `.github/workflows/`. Install the App on the target repository, then note the App ID and installation ID and download the private key (a `.pem` file; keep it mode 0600).\n\nGive `ard serve` the App through its environment: `ARD_GITHUB_APP_ID`, `ARD_GITHUB_INSTALLATION_ID` and the key as `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` (a path) or `ARD_GITHUB_PRIVATE_KEY`.\n\n## 2. Let the App push to main\n\nard commits its own files under `.ard/<issue#>/` straight to `main`. If `main` has a ruleset, add the App to the ruleset's bypass list. Without that, pushes fail with a `GH013` ruleset rejection.\n\n## 3. Audit the bot's commits\n\nSet the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login, and add a workflow that fails when a bot commit changes anything outside a single `.ard/<issue#>/` directory. The example is [.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/blob/main/.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml). The App cannot write workflows, so you add this one yourself.\n\n## 4. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init\n```\n\n`ard init` opens a pull request with the starter `.ard/` tree: `config.yaml` and the starter role directories. It also writes `.ard/ard-init.lock`, the hash of each starter file it wrote. Review and merge it. Rerunning `ard init` after an upgrade updates only files you have not edited, never touches an edited file (it prints a diff against the starter instead) and never deletes. Then:\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\nard config check\n```\n\n`ard labels sync` creates the `ard:` labels and never deletes one. `ard config check` prints the resolved configuration and reports every problem with its file and key.\n\n## 5. Store the model credential\n\nSet `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` (a subscription token from `claude setup-token`) or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the environment of `ard serve`. By default the token wins when both are set; `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` set to `oauth` or `apikey` forces one. The value is passed to the agent job and is never logged. If you use a subscription, turn off extra usage in the account so a usage limit is not billed as overage.\n\n## 6. Start serving\n\nTry a dry run first. It logs every side effect without performing it:\n\n```sh\nard serve --dry-run\n```\n\nThen start it for real:\n\n```sh\nard serve\n```\n\n## 7. File and label a first issue\n\nFile an issue describing a small change. With the default `intake.mode: label`, add the `ard:pending` label to admit it. Within one triage interval (15 minutes by default) the lead writes `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md` and the issue gains a triage-done label. If `require_approval` is on, add `ard:approved` to let a worker start.\n\nIf nothing happens, see [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\n# Onboard another repository (#304)\n\nHow to run ard on a second repository, by hand and in order. The model is one\n`ard serve` per repository. See\n[docs/proposal.md: Rollout](../proposal.md#rollout) and\n[Configuration](../proposal.md#configuration).\n\nEach `ard serve` has its own GitHub credentials (a GitHub App installation, or a\nfine-grained PAT after #302), its own state directory and its own limits. ard\ndoes not coordinate across processes. Roles live in the repository's own\n`.ard/`.\n\n## 1. Install the GitHub App\n\nInstall the App on the target repository only. A fine-grained PAT is an\nalternative only once #302 lands; until then use the App.\n\n## 2. Initialise the repository\n\n```sh\nard init --repo <owner>/<name>\n```\n\nThis opens an `ard-init` PR. Merge it with **Squash** or a **merge commit**,\nnever a rebase merge (see the `ard init` lane in\n[Guardrails](../proposal.md#guardrails)).\n\n## 3. Sync labels\n\n```sh\nard labels sync\n```\n\n## 4. Branch protection on `main`\n\nProtect `main` with a ruleset and put the App on its bypass list (mode Always).\nThe committer pushes `.ard/<issue#>/` commits straight to `main`; see \"Branch\nprotection and the bot\" in the proposal.\n\n## 5. Copy the bot-commit audit\n\nBy hand, in the target repository (do not edit `.github/` here): copy\n`.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` from this repository, then set the\nrepository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the App's `<slug>[bot]` login.\n\n## 6. Set this serve's limits\n\n`max_pods`, `budget_usd_per_day` and `max_budget_usd` in `.ard/config.yaml` apply\nto this `ard serve` alone. When a host or cluster runs more than one, set each\nserve's limits so the **total** fits the host, the cluster and the model\nsubscription.\n\nWorked example: two repositories, A and B, share one host and one subscription.\nThe host runs 4 jobs at once and the budget for the subscription is 60 USD a day.\n\n| Setting | Repo A | Repo B | Total |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `max_pods` | 2 | 2 | 4 (the host's capacity) |\n| `budget_usd_per_day` | 30 | 30 | 60 (the daily budget) |\n| `max_budget_usd` (per job) | 5 | 5 | at most 4 jobs x 5 = 20 in flight |\n\nIf A is the busier repository, give it more of both, but keep each column's sum\nwithin the total.\n\n**Token caps.** ard has no token-cap setting today, and this runbook does not\ninvent one. Divide the subscription's token allowance through `max_pods` and the\ndollar caps above. Each serve records a usage limit on its own, so one serve\nhitting the subscription limit does not stop the other until it hits it too.\n\n## 7. Dry run, soak, go live\n\n```sh\nard serve --dry-run\n```\n\nDry-run keeps its own state in `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/` and writes\nintended effects to `effects.jsonl`. Soak and read the ledger, then start\n`ard serve` without the flag.\n\nIn the cluster, add an entry for the repository to the instances list in the\ndeployment repository (check the key name in that repository's docs). Live agent\njobs in the cluster need #54; until it lands, run the cluster instance in\ndry-run only.\n\n## 8. Optional: a shared roles repository\n\n`roles_repo: {repo: <owner>/<name>, ref: <ref>}` in `.ard/config.yaml` loads\nroles from a shared repository, and the repository's own role of the same name\nwins. Pin a full commit SHA. The shared roles repository is tracked in #59; use\nthis only where it is available. See\n[Configuration](../proposal.md#configuration).\ndocscheck.go\ndocscheck_test.go","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1d44c60b-12bd-4f61-bd0f-b820cbda4621","uuid":"1e69f3c5-1c1e-44cc-906d-977a81f3812a","timestamp":"2026-11-25T11:41:33.284Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# ard — automated repository development\n\nar